
## Linux History

### The Birth of Linux

In 1991, **Linus Torvalds**, a computer science student at the University of Helsinki in Finland, started a personal project to create his own operating system kernel.

A **kernel** is the core component of an operating system that manages communication between software and hardware. Linus initially developed the kernel as a hobby project, but it quickly attracted the attention of developers around the world.

As the project grew, Linus combined the kernel with various tools and utilities required to build a complete operating system. This project eventually became known as **Linux**.

---

### The GNU General Public License (GPL)

In 1992, Linux was released under the **GNU General Public License (GPL)**, a license created by the **Free Software Foundation (FSF)**.

The GPL allows anyone to:

- Use the software freely
- Study the source code
- Modify the software
- Share and distribute copies

This open-source approach encouraged developers worldwide to contribute to Linux, making it one of the largest collaborative software projects in history.

---

### Rise of Linux Distributions

The Linux kernel alone is not a complete operating system. To create a fully functional system, it was combined with tools and utilities from the **GNU Project**.

This combination led to the creation of complete operating systems known as **Linux Distributions (Distros)**.

Some popular Linux distributions include:

- Ubuntu
- Debian
- Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL)
- CentOS
- Fedora
- Arch Linux

These distributions began appearing in the mid-1990s and played a major role in making Linux accessible to users and organizations worldwide.

